{"ts": 1789777523.939753, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Good \u2014 the signs are coherent (all-flip gives exactly \u22120.803, confirming sign consistency), and each individual flip degrades the result substantially. The signal isn't a sign-fitting artifact.\n\nNow the key question: what will this actually do out of sample? Let me quantify the selection bias rather than just eyeballing it.\n\n**The overfitting correction.** I ran 66 evaluations and picked the maximum. The in-sample Sharpe of 0.803 decomposes into true signal plus selection noise. With n=5000, the standard error on an estimated Sharpe is \u2248 \u221a(252/5000) \u2248 0.224.\n\nThe tell-tale evidence of selection inflation is the flatness I noticed: the {5,20}+X family spans 0.69\u20130.80 across *nine* different third features, including ones with near-zero standalone signal. If the third feature were contributing real, distinct alpha, I'd expect the choice to matter much more. Instead, f0 winning by ~0.1 over f31/f36 is roughly a half standard error \u2014 comfortably within noise. So a good chunk of the 0.803 \u2212 0.701 = 0.10 gain from adding f0 is likely luck.\n\nApplying a shrinkage estimate: for the max of ~15 loosely-correlated candidate triples in the top family, expected selection inflation is on the order of 0.15\u20130.25. The genuinely defensible core is the f5+f20 pair (0.701), which was itself selected from ~20 pairs and carries maybe 0.10 of inflation.\n\nThat puts my central estimate for true OOS Sharpe around **0.55\u20130.60**, meaningfully below the 0.803 in-sample figure.\n\nTwo additional haircuts I'll apply:\n- **Sign/structure risk is low** \u2014 the all-flip symmetry check confirms the directional structure is real, so I don't fear a sign reversal.\n- **Regime risk is real but unquantified** \u2014 in-sample covers one sample path; feature relationships can decay. This widens my uncertainty rather than shifting the center much.\n\nLet me set the uncertainty band. My SD needs to cover: estimation error (~0.22), residual selection-bias uncertainty (I don't know the true inflation precisely, ~0.15), and regime/decay risk (~0.10). Combining in quadrature: \u221a(0.22\u00b2 + 0.15\u00b2 + 0.10\u00b2) \u2248 0.29. I'll use **0.30** \u2014 honest width, not false precision.\n\nI'll submit the f5+f20+f0 triple, since even after shrinkage it dominates the pair, but with a predicted mean well below the in-sample number."}
